-- title:
--   Re-gluing by c+rc' shifts the formal group by w+rw'
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime, $B$ is an Artinian local ring whose residue field $k = \mathrm{ResidueField}\,B$ is algebraically closed, and $B_1$ is a $B$-algebra. The *small surjection* hypotheses are: the structure map $B \to B_1$ is surjective (`hπ`), its kernel $J = \ker(B \to B_1)$ is nilpotent (`hker`), satisfies $J \cdot \mathfrak m_B = 0$ (`hsmall`) and $J \subseteq \mathfrak m_B$ (`hI`); moreover $q$ is nilpotent in $B$ (`hq`).
--
--   Over $B_1$ there is a scheme $A_1$ with structure morphism $f_1 : A_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} B_1$, a relative group law $L_1$ on $f_1$ (functorial multiplication, unit and inverse on $T$-points over $\operatorname{Spec} B_1$, natural in $T$) which is commutative (`hc₁`), and $h_1$, the bundle asserting that $f_1$ is smooth, proper, with connected fibres and carrying a relative group law. A commutative two-dimensional formal group $F$ over $B$ is given, together with a system of formal coordinates $\theta_1$ for $f_1$ in two variables — assigning to each $B_1$-algebra $C$ and each nilpotent pair $s \in C^2$ a point of $f_1$ over $\operatorname{Spec} C$ — such that `hθ₁` holds: $\theta_1$ is natural in $C$, and for every ideal $J'$ of $C$ with $J'^{n+1} = 0$ it parametrises bijectively the $J'$-infinitesimal points and converts the truncated group law of $F \otimes_B B_1$ into $L_1$. Two further hypotheses on this object are: `hinf₁`, that every point $P$ of $f_1$ over a $B_1$-algebra $C$ killed by some $q^m$ is $J'$-infinitesimal for some nilpotent ideal $J' \subseteq C$; and `hheight`, that for every field $\kappa$ and every surjective ring homomorphism $\rho : B \to \kappa$ the quotient of $\kappa[[x_1,x_2]]$ by the ideal generated by the $q$-th iterate series of $F \otimes_\rho \kappa$ has $\kappa$-dimension $q^4$.
--
--   A finite-dimensional $k$-vector space $V$, carrying compatible $B$-module and opposite $k$-module structures, is given together with an injective $B$-linear map $\iota : V \to B$ whose image is exactly $J$ viewed as a $B$-submodule (`hι`, `hιI`).
--
--   The base object is a bare deformation $D_0$ of $(f_1, L_1)$ to $B$: a scheme $D_0.A$ with structure morphism $D_0.f$ to $\operatorname{Spec} B$, a commutative relative group law $D_0.L$, the abelian-scheme property bundle for $D_0.f$, and a morphism $D_0.g : A_1 \to D_0.A$ making $f_1$ the base change of $D_0.f$ along $B \to B_1$ and compatible with the group laws; $D_0.f$ is separated. Further data: an ordered finite affine cover $\mathcal U$ of $D_0.A$ and a distinguished index $i_0$; a section $e_0 : \operatorname{Spec} B \to \mathcal U.U\,i_0$ whose composite with the open immersion is the unit section of $D_0.L$ (`he₀`); the corresponding section $e_1$ of the base-changed chart over the special fibre $A_k = D_0.A \times_{\operatorname{Spec} B} \operatorname{Spec} k$, whose composite with the open immersion is the unit section of the base-changed group law (`he₁`); and a family $\sigma$ of ring isomorphisms $k \otimes_B \Gamma(D_0.A, \mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s) \cong \Gamma(A_k, (\mathcal U_k).\mathrm{inter}\,s)$, one for each index $s$ of a pairwise intersection, normalised by `hσ₁` (on elements $1 \otimes x$ it agrees with pullback along the first projection followed by restriction) and `hσ₂` (on elements $a \otimes 1$ it is the structure map of $k$). Finally, $G_0$ is a deformation of $F \otimes_B B_1$ to $B$ (a formal group over $B$ reducing to $F \otimes_B B_1$) with commutative law, and $\theta_0$ is a system of formal coordinates for $D_0.L$ with formal group $G_0.F$ (`hθ₀`) lifting $\theta_1$ in the sense that $(\theta_1\,C\,s)$ followed by $D_0.g$ equals $(\theta_0\,C\,s)$ for all nilpotent tuples over $B$- and $B_1$-algebras $C$ (`hl₀`).
--
--   A scalar $r \in k$ is given, and two elements $c, c'$ of the module of point derivations of $\Gamma(A_k, (\mathcal U_k).U\,i_0)$ at the unit point determined by $e_1$, with values in $\operatorname{Hom}_k(V^\vee, C^1(\mathcal U_k, \mathcal O))$: that is, $k$-linear maps $D$ on the sections of the chart with $D(ab) = \mathrm{ev}(a)\,D(b) + \mathrm{ev}(b)\,D(a)$, where $\mathrm{ev}$ is evaluation at the unit and $C^1$ denotes the degree-one Čech cochains of the structure presheaf of modules on the cover $\mathcal U_k$ of the special fibre. The hypotheses `hc` and `hc'` state that all values $c\,a\,\xi$, respectively $c'\,a\,\xi$ ($a$ a section, $\xi \in V^\vee$), are Čech $1$-cocycles, i.e. lie in the kernel of the degree-one differential.
--
--   Three families $\tau, \tau', \tau''$ of automorphisms of the pairwise intersections $\mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s$ are given, and three bare deformations $D, D', D''$ of $(f_1, L_1)$ to $B$ obtained from $D_0$ by re-gluing along them: `hD`, `hD'`, `hD''` assert `IsRegluingBy`, i.e. each $\tau_s$ commutes with the structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} B$ and fixes the restriction of $D_0.g$, and the target carries a family of open immersions of the charts $\mathcal U.U\,i$ over $D_0.f$ which cover it, are compatible with $D_0.g$, and overlap through the prescribed $\tau_s$. The hypotheses `hτ`, `hτ'`, `hτ''` identify the tangent coordinates of these re-gluings with $c$, $c'$ and $c + r\,c'$ respectively: for each $s$ there is a map $c_s$ from the sections of the chart $(\mathcal U_k).U\,i_0$ to $\operatorname{Hom}_k(V^\vee, k \otimes_B \Gamma(D_0.A, \mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s))$ which is a system of tangent coordinates, relative to $J$, $V$ and $\iota$, for the pair consisting of the canonical morphism $\operatorname{Spec} \Gamma(D_0.A, \mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s) \to D_0.A$ and its twist by the relevant overlap automorphism (that predicate requires a morphism from the spectrum of the thickening $(k \otimes_B \Gamma) \otimes_k (k \oplus V)$ to the special fibre over the base, a lift of its translate by the base-changed group law into the chart, that the composite with the first projection be a tangent of the given pair, and that $c_s$ be the associated tangent-coordinate function); and, transported by $\sigma_s$, these coordinates are the $s$-components of $c$, of $c'$, and of $c + r\,c'$ respectively.
--
--   Finally, $G, G', G''$ are deformations of $F \otimes_B B_1$ to $B$ with commutative laws, equipped with formal coordinates $\theta, \theta', \theta''$ for $D.L, D'.L, D''.L$ with these formal groups (`hθ`, `hθ'`, `hθ''`) and lifting $\theta_1$ along $D.g$, $D'.g$, $D''.g$ (`hl`, `hl'`, `hl''`); and $w, w'$ are $k$-linear maps from $V^\vee$ to the first-order deformation space of $F \otimes_B k$ (first-order cocycles modulo coboundaries). The hypotheses `h` and `h'` assert $\mathrm{IsShiftBy}\,V\,\iota\,F\,w\,G_0\,G$ and $\mathrm{IsShiftBy}\,V\,\iota\,F\,w'\,G_0\,G'$.
--
--   The conclusion is $\mathrm{IsShiftBy}\,V\,\iota\,F\,(w + r\,w')\,G_0\,G''$: there exist an integer $n$, vectors $v_0,\dots,v_{n-1} \in V$, first-order cocycles $z_0,\dots,z_{n-1}$ for $F \otimes_B k$, and families $zl_i$ of two power series in $\mathrm{MvPowerSeries}(\mathrm{Fin}\,2 \oplus \mathrm{Fin}\,2, B)$ such that the reduction of $zl_i$ modulo $\mathfrak m_B$ is $z_i$ componentwise; for every $\xi \in V^\vee$ one has $(w + r\,w')(\xi) = \sum_i \xi(v_i) \cdot [z_i]$ in the first-order deformation space; and for each component $l$ the $l$-th power series of $G''.F$ equals that of $G_0.F$ plus $\sum_i \iota(v_i) \cdot zl_i(l)$.
--
--   This is the additivity statement underlying the linearity of the Kodaira–Spencer correspondence between the Čech class of the coordinate cocycle used to re-glue a bare deformation and the resulting shift of its formal group: re-gluing by the affine combination $c + r\,c'$ of two tangent-coordinate cocycles shifts the formal group by $w + r\,w'$. It is used by [`GoodReductionJacobian.BareDeformation.exists_linearMap_pointDerivations_forall_isShiftBy`](thm.html#GoodReductionJacobian.BareDeformation.exists_linearMap_pointDerivations_forall_isShiftBy), where the assignment is assembled into a $k$-linear map on point derivations.
